WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.902
Hello! I'd like to show you how
Microsoft's Decision

00:00:03.902 --> 00:00:07.804
One model can help with customer
conversations and a

00:00:07.804 --> 00:00:11.705
busy inbox. It answers specific questions
about text, returning

00:00:11.705 --> 00:00:15.607
probabilities, categories, or scores
rather than writing a reply.

00:00:15.607 --> 00:00:17.775
We'll use recorded Azure examples.

00:00:18.175 --> 00:00:21.424
Imagine you're supporting this broadband
customer. Their bill has

00:00:21.424 --> 00:00:24.673
doubled, and they're getting frustrated as
they try to

00:00:24.673 --> 00:00:27.922
fix it. The challenge is noticing that
frustration and

00:00:27.922 --> 00:00:31.171
cancellation intent while the conversation
is happening, not just

00:00:31.171 --> 00:00:33.337
labeling the call after it's over.

00:00:33.738 --> 00:00:36.924
This is where Decision One fits. We ask:

00:00:36.924 --> 00:00:40.111
is the customer talking about cancelling?
What's the

00:00:40.111 --> 00:00:43.298
issue? How upset are they? Here, the panel

00:00:43.298 --> 00:00:46.485
shows billing, an angry mood, and a
cancellation

00:00:46.485 --> 00:00:49.672
signal. Those signals could help an
application flag

00:00:49.672 --> 00:00:52.063
the conversation for an agent's attention.

00:00:52.462 --> 00:00:55.215
Now let's see how the conversation ends.
As

00:00:55.215 --> 00:00:57.967
the agent sorts out the billing problem,
the

00:00:57.967 --> 00:01:00.719
mood improves and the cancellation signal
changes. The

00:01:00.719 --> 00:01:03.471
useful result is a structured view that
updates

00:01:03.471 --> 00:01:06.223
with the conversation, so an application
doesn't have

00:01:06.223 --> 00:01:08.975
to wait for an end of call summary.

00:01:09.375 --> 00:01:13.203
The second problem is a busy support
inbox. Forty

00:01:13.203 --> 00:01:17.030
messages arrive in a burst: damaged
products, shipping issues,

00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:20.858
and routine questions. Before anyone can
help, each message

00:01:20.858 --> 00:01:24.685
needs a destination and a priority. Doing
that sorting

00:01:24.685 --> 00:01:28.087
manually adds another step for the support
team.

00:01:28.487 --> 00:01:31.816
Let's put those same forty messages
through both lanes.

00:01:31.816 --> 00:01:35.144
Each message gets a department, an urgency
level, and

00:01:35.144 --> 00:01:38.473
a signal for churn risk. Now the support
team

00:01:38.473 --> 00:01:41.801
has a structured view to work from, rather
than

00:01:41.801 --> 00:01:43.650
a pile of unclassified text.

00:01:44.050 --> 00:01:47.205
At this point, Decision One has classified
all forty

00:01:47.205 --> 00:01:50.361
messages, while the baseline still has
work outstanding. The

00:01:50.361 --> 00:01:53.516
appeal isn't just a number on the chart.
Routine

00:01:53.516 --> 00:01:56.672
sorting could happen earlier, so the team
can focus

00:01:56.672 --> 00:01:58.425
on customers who need attention.

00:01:58.825 --> 00:02:02.040
One thing to keep in mind: Decision One
has

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:05.254
eight workers here, and the baseline has
four. That

00:02:05.254 --> 00:02:08.469
affects the comparison, so I wouldn't
treat this run

00:02:08.469 --> 00:02:11.683
as a universal speed claim. While both
lanes finish,

00:02:11.683 --> 00:02:14.898
notice that we keep each message's
decision, not just

00:02:14.898 --> 00:02:18.112
a total. That makes the result easier to
inspect.

00:02:18.513 --> 00:02:21.416
By the end, both lanes have processed all

00:02:21.416 --> 00:02:24.320
forty messages. In this recording,
messages finished in

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:27.223
under a second on average in the Decision

00:02:27.223 --> 00:02:30.127
One lane, and around ten seconds in the

00:02:30.127 --> 00:02:33.031
baseline lane, with those different worker
limits. Each

00:02:33.031 --> 00:02:35.934
message now has a department and urgency,
and

00:02:35.934 --> 00:02:38.475
potential churn cases are flagged for
review.

00:02:38.875 --> 00:02:42.021
So those are two examples: spotting
changing signals

00:02:42.021 --> 00:02:45.167
during a conversation, and classifying
incoming work. The

00:02:45.167 --> 00:02:48.313
same pattern can support policy checks or
routing

00:02:48.313 --> 00:02:51.458
decisions. A language model can still
write the

00:02:51.458 --> 00:02:54.604
response. Decision One provides the
structured signals that

00:02:54.604 --> 00:02:57.750
help an application decide what to do
next.
